-- title:
--   Descent of symmetric 2-cocycles along a finite endomorphism
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field and let $F$ be an $n$-dimensional formal group law over $k$, i.e. an $n$-tuple $F_i$ of power series in the $2n$ variables indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\,n \oplus \mathrm{Fin}\,n$ with zero constant term, with linear coefficients $\delta_{ij}$ in both the left and the right variables, and satisfying the associativity identity $F(F(X,Y),Z)=F(X,F(Y,Z))$; assume furthermore that $F$ is commutative, in the sense that interchanging the left and right blocks of variables fixes each $F_i$. Let $\varphi = (\varphi_i)_{i<n}$ be an $n$-tuple of power series in $n$ variables, each with zero constant term, which is an endomorphism of $F$: for every $i$, $\varphi_i(F(X,Y)) = F_i(\varphi(X),\varphi(Y))$, where on the right $\varphi$ is substituted into the left, resp. right, block of variables. Assume the quotient of $k[\![X_1,\dots,X_n]\!]$ by the ideal generated by the $\varphi_i$ is a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space. Let $\Gamma$ be a power series in the $2n$ variables which is a symmetric normalised additive $2$-cocycle for $F$: $\Gamma$ has zero constant term, is invariant under interchanging the two blocks of variables, and satisfies $\Gamma(F(X,Y),Z)+\Gamma(X,Y) = \Gamma(X,F(Y,Z))+\Gamma(Y,Z)$. Let $g$ be a power series in $n$ variables with zero constant term such that $\Gamma(\varphi(X),\varphi(Y))$ equals the additive coboundary $\partial_F g = g(F(X,Y)) - g(X) - g(Y)$, and such that $g$ lies in the ideal generated by the $\varphi_i$. Then there exists a power series $G$ in $n$ variables with zero constant term such that $g = G(\varphi)$ and $\Gamma = \partial_F G$.
--
--   This is the descent step for symmetric $2$-cocycles along an endomorphism $\varphi$ of a commutative formal group law whose kernel is finite, the finiteness being expressed as finite-dimensionality of $k[\![X]\!]/(\varphi_1,\dots,\varphi_n)$; the case $\varphi = [m]$ is the classical divisibility argument for additive $2$-cocycles. It is used in the construction of extensions of special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules in the Cerednik–Drinfeld setting, and its proof rests on the finiteness and flatness of $k[\![X]\!]$ over the image of substitution by $\varphi$ provided by [`MvPowerSeries.finite_flat_exists_basis_substAlgHom_of_finite_quotient`](thm.html#MvPowerSeries.finite_flat_exists_basis_substAlgHom_of_finite_quotient).
